00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  the government launches the uk's first  hydrogen strategy to transform the way  we power our lives but how green is it  the us declares the first ever water  shortage at the country's largest  reservoir as drought pushes water levels  to a record low  and the environmentalists cleaning up  lakes in india  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show and we start today at  our data dashboard and you can see here  on the top line where electricity in  great britain is currently coming from  and you can see there that 33 is coming  from fossil fuels 49 from renewables and  18 from nuclear  well hydrogen isn't on there because  currently only around one percent of  electricity is produced by it but that  could soon change the government's new  hydrogen strategy has been published  today suggesting that hydrogen could  make up one third of our energy mix by  2050. their proposal aims to burn  hydrogen as an alternative to oil gas  and coal to encourage the use of low  carbon fuel and cut greenhouse gas  emissions but campaigners say the new  plans to make hydrogen from fossil fuels  is not completely green well our science  correspondent thomas moore is here to  explain thomas  hydrogen is the invisible fuel of the  future but how it's made really matters  to the planet and the production method  has a color code gray hydrogen makes up  the majority of current production it's  made from natural gas or other fossil  fuels using a process called steam  methane reforming and it generates  carbon dioxide and methane emissions  blue hydrogen is made in the same way  but technology is used to capture most  but not all of the carbon dioxide  emissions for storage underground or  under the sea in theory this should mean  it is less polluting than grey hydrogen  but a recent study has found that the  production of blue hydrogen is far from  green due to the release of some methane  green hydrogen is zero carbon it's made  by using wind or solar to split water  into hydrogen and oxygen gases but it's  currently an inefficient process and  there's nowhere near enough spare  renewable capacity at the moment to make  the huge amounts of hydrogen we will  need in its new hydrogen strategy the  government wants to incentivize low  carbon production and to me the  definition is considering a target of 54  to 72 grams of carbon dioxide for every  kilowatt of energy roughly a third of  the emissions from burning natural gas  now many environmentalists would argue  it's a pretty soft target and it's  better to find a way of making hydrogen  with zero emissions to sustainably fuel  heavy industry buses lorries as well as  heating homes and thomas what are the  cost implications for consumers if we  were to move towards hydrogen so  the consultation released today says  that the the costs could be loaded onto  consumers through energy bills just as  they were in the early days of wind  energy when it's very expensive but as  it was scaled up the efficiencies came  and the cost of wind energy is now about  the same as any other fossil fuel  so that's the hope with hydrogen that by  putting the load on to consumers at the  beginning it would slowly come down  ultimately hydrogen would be an  extremely cheap way of fueling the  future so a cost for the consumers now  but probably not in the long term thomas  thank you  well don't forget you can see a more  detailed breakdown of the real  power mix in great britain on our  website head over to skynews.com  climate  in today's other climate news several  people have needed to be rescued from  flood waters in germany after sudden and  heavy rain the flooding caused a wooden  bridge in the valley of hell in bavaria  to be swept away severe weather  conditions also led to a state of  emergency in northern germany where a  storm destroyed more than 50 homes  and in france thousands of people have  been evacuated to escape wildfires  firefighters are tackling fires in the  french riviera that are threatening  homes and tourist spots it follows  fierce forest fires that have swept  across northern africa and europe in  recent weeks  and a chalk stream that feeds into the  norfolk broads will be the focus of a  1.6 million pound conservation project  the national trust who are leading the  project said there are just 200 chalk  streams left in the world it's hoped the  project that will create corridors for  wildlife will help britain's fastest  declining mammal the waterfall  now to the u.s where officials have  declared the first ever water shortage  at lake mead the country's largest  reservoir a prolonged drought across the  west has led to record low water levels  there so let's take a look at what's  happening then today now this is the u.s  drought monitor and you can see here  that large areas in the west are bright  red and dark red now these are the  places experiencing extreme and  exceptional drought  lake mead is in one of those red areas  running through arizona and nevada water  levels are at the lowest point since the  1930s when the reservoir was first  filled so what's going on  [Music]  [Music]  there's no doubt that climate change is  real we're experiencing it every day in  the colorado river basin and in other  basins in the west  [Music]  every community  every sector every industry that uses  colorado river water must do more to  conserve and protect this critical water  resource  well joining me now is jennifer pitt  colorado river program director for the  environmental organization the national  audubon society  welcome to you so lake mead's water  levels have been declared critically low  for the first time  so how concerning is that what impact  will that have am i right in thinking  that agriculture and drinking water are  perhaps amongst the most pressing  concerns  uh  this is a serious concern  but it's not unexpected  uh we've been watching the lake levels  decline for  most of the last 20 years i think the  biggest concern is not what we do about  shortages to  water users as you said to farmers and  perhaps some others in the short term  but  we have no reason to think that these  drought conditions are going to ease up  in the long term  we hope that they do but the warming uh  means that we could be facing  uh  these kind of shortages year after year  and that's the biggest concern  well yes and what impact do the  shortages have  uh well  this year or the coming year what we  expect is for the shortages to  largely be born by farmers in central  arizona and in mexico  and so there will be some reduction in  agricultural productivity in those  regions  and can anything be done about it  well in arizona they are working on a  mitigation plan that includes  uh  releasing water from local reservoirs  some  market transfers where actually people  who use water who are willing to  forgo their uses are selling it to allow  the farmers to have access to some of it  and  they are also going to allow those  farmers to return to pumping fossil  groundwater which of course will work in  the short term but is not a sustainable  solution  and what about the impact on wildlife in  the area  well uh we don't yet know we haven't  seen uh reductions of these kinds in the  water delivery so we haven't seen the  river flowing in the way it will be next  year but we do have concern for habitat  areas along the banks of the colorado  river that depend on the water flowing  by  to remain healthy and so we will be  keeping a close eye on it and as i said  these are  conditions that we expect to persist and  so as we  get a better understanding of  the impact on habitat areas it may be  necessary  to  make some investments to  improve the habitat areas such that they  can remain healthy at lower water levels  jennifer pitt thanks very much indeed  thank you  and the drought in california is  threatening the state's 6 billion almond  industry which produces about 80 percent  of the world's almonds more growers are  expected to abandon their orchards as  water becomes scarce and expensive  now many of india's waterways are  polluted filled with plastic waste and  raw sewage one man working to change  that is aaron krishnamurthy in 2010 he  quit his job at google to set up the  environmentalist foundation of india  since then the organization has restored  more than 150 freshwater lakes and ponds  across the country  lake is a magical world in itself to  abuse and exploit a lake happens only  when we don't understand its  significance  we don't understand that every lake or a  pond is a habitat in itself  which houses frogs snakes crabs turtles  birds so many different life forms  human beings directly depend on  underground water source so a lake or a  pond is necessary to replenish these  groundwater resources  [Music]  lakes and ponds also play a big role in  localized temperature regulation lakes  and ponds need to be cleaned so that it  doesn't cause any waterboard diseases to  the neighboring individuals  [Music]  we think we're superior because we can  walk straight and talk around and we  fail to understand that we're just one  of the several species in this planet  it's not about protecting the planet  anymore it's about our life our  livelihoods and from the urban ridge to  the urban poor the divine on who gets  affected by climate change  one thing which i realized is  everybody wants to be good everybody  wants to participate in planet  conservation efforts they just don't  know where to begin efi or other  organizations are platforms through  which people are realizing and living  their planet life  [Music]  and finally over half a million seeds  have been collected from healthy  seagrass meadows to be planted the ocean  conservation trust who are leading the  project plan to plant a total of four  hectares in the solent maritime special  area of conservation seagrass is vital  for marine life and can absorb large  amounts of carbon  well that's everything from us for today  coming up tomorrow meet six-year-old  climate activist alicia guadia who's  riding her scooter to save rainforests  thanks for watching see you then  [Music]

